{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a medieval ruler of the County of Tripoli in the Kingdom of Jerusalem from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was a member of the Anjou family and was known for his military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate. \n\n* **Early Life and Reign **\n\nRaymond III was the son of Pons II Count of Tripoli and his wife Gersenda of Béthune. He succeeded his father as the Count of Tripoli in 1152 at the age of seven. He was a minor when he took power and therefore his mother acted as regent until he came of age in 1162. Raymond III was educated in Italy and France and was trained in the military arts.\n\n* **Military Campaigns **\n\nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and expanded the County of Tripoli through a series of military campaigns. He captured several key cities in the region including the port city of Sidon. In 1169 Raymond III led an expedition against the Fatimid Caliphate but was unsuccessful in his attempts to capture the city of Tripoli. He did capture the city of Ma'arrat al-Numan but was forced to return it.\n\n* **Relationship with Saladin **\n\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with Saladin the Ayyubid ruler of Egypt and Syria. While the two leaders were bitter enemies they also maintained a cordial relationship. In 1172 Raymond III and Saladin negotiated a treaty that allowed Christian pilgrims to visit Jerusalem and the Holy Sepulchre. However this treaty was short lived and hostilities between the two leaders resumed.\n\nRaymond III was known for his piety and supported the Crusaders in their struggle against Saladin. He died on October 5 1187 at the Battle of Hattin while fighting against Saladin's forces.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.001581966644152999}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001299296854995191}, {"token": " Coun